Q: Does @Transactional still roll back if the exception is handled with try-catch?

Short answer: Usually NO.
If you catch the exception inside the @Transactional method and do not rethrow (and do not mark rollback),
Spring sees a normal successful return → transaction COMMITS.

This is a very common Spring interview trap.

────────────────────────────────────────
1. How Spring decides commit vs rollback

Spring’s transaction proxy wraps the method.

- Exception propagates out of the @Transactional method
  → by default ROLLBACK for RuntimeException / Error
- Method returns normally (no exception leaving the method)
  → COMMIT

So try-catch matters because catching can hide the exception from the proxy.

Default rollback rules:
| Exception type              | Default behavior      |
| --------------------------- | --------------------- |
| RuntimeException / Error    | ROLLBACK              |
| Checked Exception           | COMMIT (unless `rollbackFor`) |

────────────────────────────────────────
2. Normal case (exception escapes) → ROLLBACK

@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    accountRepo.debit(100);

    throw new RuntimeException("Error");

    // credit never reached
}

Result: RuntimeException leaves the method → ROLLBACK (debit undone).

────────────────────────────────────────
3. try-catch without rethrow → COMMIT (danger)

@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(100);
        throw new RuntimeException("Error");
        // accountRepo.credit(100); never reached
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println("Handled");
        // swallowed — method ends normally
    }
}

Result:
- Exception caught inside method
- Proxy does NOT see a failure
- Transaction COMMITS
- debit may be saved, credit never ran → inconsistency

────────────────────────────────────────
4. Correct approach A — rethrow

@Transactional
public void transfer() {
    try {
        accountRepo.debit(100);
        throw new RuntimeException("Error");
    } catch (Exception e) {
        log.error("transfer failed", e);
        throw e; // must propagate
    }
}

Result: exception reaches proxy → ROLLBACK.

────────────────────────────────────────
5. Correct approach B — mark rollback only

If you must catch and not rethrow (e.g. return an error DTO):

catch (Exception e) {
    TransactionAspectSupport
            .currentTransactionStatus()
            .setRollbackOnly();
    // return error response / handle without rethrowing
}

Result: method can return normally, but Spring still rolls back on completion.

────────────────────────────────────────
6. Best practice

- Prefer let exceptions propagate from transactional service methods.
- If you catch: log + rethrow, OR setRollbackOnly().
- Don’t swallow business/DB failures inside @Transactional.

────────────────────────────────────────
7. Interview one-liner

If an exception is caught and handled inside a @Transactional method and not rethrown,
Spring treats the method as successful and commits — unless you explicitly call setRollbackOnly().
Rollback happens when the failure is visible to the transaction proxy (exception escapes) or rollback is marked manually.
